+# Zero-byte thumbnails in staging
+
+Staging produced zero-byte thumbnail objects at roughly 1 upload in 40.
+A `time.sleep(2)` now sits in `render.py`'s `main()` before the upload call
+and the symptom has not recurred.
+
+**Status: symptom suppressed, cause unknown.** The sleep is not a fix and
+nothing below identifies the mechanism. Read this before changing
+`render.py`, before removing the sleep, and before re-opening any
+hypothesis listed here as ruled out.
+
+## The central open question
+
+In `main()` the sleep sits between `blob` being computed and the PUT.
+`blob` is already fully in memory when the sleep begins, so the sleep
+cannot change the bytes being uploaded. Whatever it does, it does by
+delaying the PUT alone.
+
+That PUT, in `upload()`, is also the process's only network call, and
+`worker.py`'s `main()` runs one `render.py` process per job, so each job
+performs its own fresh DNS resolution of `cdn.example` with no connection
+reuse. A 2-second delay before the first resolution in a short-lived
+process is exactly the shape of change that would mask a resolver problem
+at process startup — which is why Priya's note about the new runner image
+shipping a different DNS resolver is the ranked live hypothesis. Nobody
+has checked it against thumbd.
+
+It does not yet explain the symptom. A resolution failure raises, and
+`urllib` raises `HTTPError` on any 4xx/5xx, so both would crash the
+process and surface through `worker.py`'s `check=True` as a loud
+`CalledProcessError`. A zero-byte object stored under a 2xx response
+remains unaccounted for. Anyone picking this up needs a mechanism that
+produces *silence*, not an error.
+
+## Ruled out: any render-side origin
+
+`thumbnail()` cannot return empty bytes. It either returns a non-empty
+WEBP or raises — there is no silent-empty path, for truncated input or for
+valid-but-degenerate input:
+
+| Input to `thumbnail()` | Result |
+| --- | --- |
+| complete 5150-byte PNG | 172-byte WEBP |
+| 90% / 50% / 25% / 5% of that PNG | `OSError: image file is truncated` |
+| 0.1% of it, or 0 bytes | `PIL.UnidentifiedImageError` |
+| 1x1, 2x3, 1x5000, 5000x1 RGB | 44-66 byte WEBP (smallest seen: 44) |
+| modes L, RGBA, P, 1, I;16 | 150-194 byte WEBP |
+| 3-frame GIF | 150-byte WEBP |
+| 0x0 | cannot be encoded at all: `ValueError: cannot write empty image` |
+
+So `blob` is always non-empty by the time `main()` reaches the sleep, and a
+bad source crashes loudly and exits nonzero rather than uploading nothing.
+
+That table was produced with a current Pillow outside the runner image, and
+the repo pins no Pillow version, so the exact exception types may differ
+there. The conclusion does not depend on them: the rows that raise would at
+most stop raising (if something in staging sets
+`ImageFile.LOAD_TRUNCATED_IMAGES`), which yields a partial *non-empty*
+thumbnail, not an empty one.
+
+**This puts the zero-byte object at or after the PUT, not in rendering.**
+A future agent should spend its effort on `upload()` and the network path,
+and should not re-examine the cache file, the decode, or the encoder. Both
+the partial-read theory and the source-corruption theory are closed.
+
+## Fixed, and never the cause: `render.py` read unlocked
+
+`worker.py` holds a lock and `render.py` did not. The two paths were never
+symmetric, contrary to what was assumed during the investigation — so
+"`worker.py` reads it the same way" was false, and "`worker.py` never
+failed" is explained by its lock being load-bearing rather than by any
+absence of hazard.
+
+`worker.py`'s `source_is_complete()` takes `LOCK_SH` before sizing the
+file. The upload handler holds `LOCK_EX` across its write, so that call
+blocks until the write completes. `render.py`'s `source_bytes()` called
+`read_bytes()` with no lock at all. Measured against a writer holding
+`LOCK_EX` while writing 4000 bytes in four flushed chunks:
+
+```
+worker.py  LOCK_SH  : blocked 0.70s, then saw size=4000 of 4000
+render.py  no lock  : blocked 0.00s, then read 2000 of 4000 bytes
+render.py  patched  : blocked 0.70s, then read 4000 of 4000 bytes
+```
+
+`source_bytes()` now takes `LOCK_SH`, the third line above. This closed a
+real defect but changes nothing about the zero-byte rate: per the table
+above, a partial read crashes rather than uploading nothing. Do not expect
+this to have retired the bug.
+
+## Confirmed, unrelated: a skipped id is dropped permanently
+
+`worker.py`'s `main()` skips an id whose source is not yet non-empty, and
+`queue.txt` is read once and never written back, so a skipped id is never
+retried. An upload that loses that race gets no thumbnail at all. That is
+a *missing* object, not a zero-byte one — do not conflate the two when
+reading staging reports.
+
+## Confirmed, unrelated: one crash abandons the rest of the run
+
+`worker.py`'s `main()` wraps nothing in `except`. Its `subprocess.run(...,
+check=True)` raises `CalledProcessError` straight out of `main()`, so a
+single `render.py` crash — including one caused by the resolver hypothesis
+above — ends the whole drain, and every id after the failing one in
+`queue.txt` is abandoned with no message at all. The skip path at least
+prints to stderr; this one does not.
+
+This bears directly on the counting in step 2 below: a drain that aborts
+early undercounts clean uploads without signalling that the count is
+partial. Check `worker.py`'s own exit status before trusting any
+"no zero-byte objects seen" run as a complete count.
+
+## The reproduction arithmetic
+
+Thirty local runs of `render.py` is not evidence that the bug is absent.
+At 1-in-40, thirty runs have a 46.8% chance of showing nothing even if the
+defect is fully present. Detection needs **119 runs for 95% confidence**
+and **182 for 99%**.
+
+Running `render.py` directly also bypasses both prime suspects: it skips
+`worker.py`'s lock gate, and on a laptop it skips the runner image whose
+resolver is the leading hypothesis. A local no-repro carries close to no
+information about this bug.
+
+The same arithmetic governs the claim that the sleep fixed it. "It has not
+happened since" is only meaningful against a count of clean uploads: 119
+consecutive clean staging uploads for 95% confidence, 182 for 99%. Until
+someone has that count, the suppression is unconfirmed too.
+
+## What would settle it
+
+One instrumented staging run, in this order:
+
+1. Log, per job, the resolved address for `cdn.example`, the PUT response
+   status, and the `Content-Length` the CDN reports storing. `upload()`
+   currently reads the response and discards it, so today a 2xx that
+   stored nothing looks identical to success. This is the single highest
+   value change available and it has not been made.
+2. With that logging in place, remove the sleep and let staging run past
+   119 uploads. Either zero-byte objects reappear with a resolved address
+   and status attached to each, or they do not and the sleep was never the
+   variable. Count only uploads from drains that exited zero — see the
+   abandoned-run hazard above.
+3. Only then decide whether the sleep stays. It currently costs 2 seconds
+   per upload, serially, on every job.
+
+Do not remove the sleep without step 1 in place; that reverts to the
+original undiagnosable state.
